  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dick was the younger son of J. Henry Dick and Julia Mollenhauer, joining two established New York industrial families. His grandfather William Dick made a substantial fortune in Brooklyn sugar refining and founded the Manufacturers National Bank. His mother’s relatives were likewise prominent in sugar, placing Adolph within a dense network of refining, banking and family trusts before he attended Yale.\n\nUnlike his older brother William, who chaired National Sugar Refining, Adolph did not pursue a similarly prominent executive career. A Mollenhauer estate settlement made him one of the family beneficiaries, and he also held Allen Winden, the family’s Long Island country estate. He enlarged the house during the 1930s and retained it within the family. His role was therefore that of an heir managing family assets with a deliberately private profile.",
    "html": "<p>Dick was the younger son of J. Henry Dick and Julia Mollenhauer, joining two established New York industrial families. His grandfather William Dick made a substantial fortune in Brooklyn sugar refining and founded the Manufacturers National Bank. His mother’s relatives were likewise prominent in sugar, placing Adolph within a dense network of refining, banking and family trusts before he attended Yale.</p>\r\n<p>Unlike his older brother William, who chaired National Sugar Refining, Adolph did not pursue a similarly prominent executive career. A Mollenhauer estate settlement made him one of the family beneficiaries, and he also held Allen Winden, the family’s Long Island country estate. He enlarged the house during the 1930s and retained it within the family. His role was therefore that of an heir managing family assets with a deliberately private profile.</p>\r\n",
